  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Britain planned an aircraft carrier made entirely of ice. This was a funded project with drawings.",
        "visual_query": "frozen canadian lake winter ice",
        "visual_fallback": "frozen lake"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The problem was the mid-Atlantic gap, a stretch of ocean no land-based aircraft could reach. U-boats hunted freely inside it.",
        "visual_query": "grey north atlantic ocean waves",
        "visual_fallback": "atlantic waves"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Geoffrey Pyke's answer was pykrete: fourteen percent wood pulp, the rest water. Frozen, it is far tougher than ice and melts slowly.",
        "visual_query": "sawdust wood pulp ice block",
        "visual_fallback": "ice block"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The design ran two thousand feet long and 2.2 million tons. A sixty-foot prototype sat on Patricia Lake in Alberta, chilled by one small motor.",
        "visual_query": "alberta mountain lake jasper shoreline",
        "visual_fallback": "mountain lake"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then long-range aircraft closed the gap, and the costings beat it. Real steel carriers were cheaper than a mountain of frozen sawdust.",
        "visual_query": "wartime aircraft carrier deck sea",
        "visual_fallback": "carrier deck"
      }
    ],
